        "viewpoint": "In the second quarter, the A-share market exhibited a volatile and divergent pattern, with policy drivers intertwining with external risks. At the beginning of the quarter, affected by the tariff war provoked by the United States, the stock market experienced significant fluctuations. As the hedging effects of policies materialized and tariff disputes gradually eased, the A-share market stabilized and recovered its previous losses.\nMarket turnover decreased compared to the first quarter, dropping below 1 trillion yuan at its lowest point. Market activity picked up in the latter half of the month, with turnover rebounding to around 1.5 trillion yuan. Northbound capital showed phased fluctuations; in the second quarter, net purchases of the banking sector exceeded 32 billion yuan, while there was a phased outflow from the technology sector. The margin financing and securities lending balance remained at around 900,000 billion yuan, indicating that the overall market risk appetite was low.\nThe Hong Kong stock market exhibited characteristics dominated by policy-driven factors intertwined with external risks and structural market conditions. The market achieved a restorative rise amid volatility, with the technology and consumption sectors serving as the core engines. Major indices all experienced varying degrees of growth. The market showed a divergent pattern of \"strong technology and weak cyclical sectors,\" with the Hang Seng Tech Index surging 17.53% in the single month of May, hitting a new high since 2024. Although it pulled back in June due to external tariff policy disturbances, the overall quarterly trend remained upward.\nThe gold market fluctuated significantly amid intertwined policy shocks and geopolitical risks. In April, impacted by the implementation of reciprocal US tariffs and escalating geopolitical risks, spot gold prices in London once broke through $3,500 per ounce, hitting a record high. Subsequently, influenced by factors such as Trump's conciliatory remarks on tariff policies and better-than-expected positive outcomes from the China-US Geneva talks, gold prices fell back to around $3,200 per ounce. International crude oil prices saw their price center shift downward under the game of supply and demand, with structural contradictions becoming prominent.\nThe bond market exhibited characteristics of intertwined policy drivers and supply-demand dynamics, with the divergence between interest rate bonds and credit bonds intensifying. The market achieved structural repair amidst volatility, led by the downward movement of interest rate bonds, while credit bond divergence intensified. Expectations of policy easing and external risk disturbances dominated the market rhythm.\nIn the second quarter, the fund adhered to the principle of balanced allocation. Among equity assets, both growth and value styles held certain positions. In terms of investment markets, it held A-share assets, Hong Kong stock assets, and US stock assets respectively. In fixed-income assets, A-shares were mainly dominated by bond ETFs. The fund strives to reduce the volatility of the entire portfolio and enhance potential returns through the low correlation of fluctuations among various markets and assets, especially when volatility is high across all investment markets.\n",
